<h2>Is Christmas Cactus Poisonous to Cats? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>Christmas cactus is not poisonous to cats. The ASPCA plant database marks it non-toxic for cats, dogs and horses, and Pet Poison Helpline also calls it a non-toxic plant. A cat that chews it may still vomit or pass soft stools for a short time, because plant material is hard for cats to digest.</p>

<h3>Is Christmas cactus toxic to cats? What the ASPCA says</h3>
<p>No. The ASPCA lists Christmas cactus (<em>Schlumbergera bridgesii</em>) as non-toxic to cats, with no toxic principle named, and gives “Easter cactus” as an extra common name. The Christmas cactus is a spineless, epiphytic cactus, meaning it grows on trees or rocks rather than in soil; NC State Extension traces its wild relatives to the coastal rainforests of southeastern Brazil and uses <em>Schlumbergera</em> × <em>buckleyi</em> for the true Christmas cactus hybrid.</p>

<h3>Is a Christmas cactus a succulent?</h3>
<p>Yes. A succulent is a plant that stores water in thick, fleshy tissue, and NC State Extension lists the Christmas cactus as both a succulent and an epiphyte. Unlike a desert cactus, it prefers bright, indirect light and soil kept moist but not soggy from spring to summer. Its flat “leaves” are spineless stem segments called cladodes.</p>

<h2>Is Christmas Cactus Poisonous to Dogs?</h2>
<p>Christmas cactus is not poisonous to dogs. The ASPCA lists it as non-toxic for dogs, and serious problems are not expected. A dog that eats a lot may still drool, vomit or have diarrhea from the fibrous plant material, and most recover without treatment.</p>

