</figure>
<p>So can dogs eat Christmas cactus? It will not poison them, but it is not food.</p>

<h3>Are Christmas cactus flowers harmful to dogs?</h3>
<p>No. The ASPCA rating covers the whole plant, so a dog that ate a Christmas cactus flower or leaf has not swallowed a poison. Expect, at most, brief vomiting or soft stools; call your vet if it continues.</p>

<h2>What Happens If a Cat Eats a Christmas Cactus?</h2>
<p>If a cat eats a Christmas cactus, usually nothing serious happens. Pet Poison Helpline says mild vomiting, nausea and diarrhea can occur, but serious toxicity is not expected. Call your vet if signs last more than a day or keep coming back.</p>
<p>According to PetMD, possible signs include vomiting, diarrhea, reduced appetite, lethargy and nausea, which may show as drooling or lip-smacking. Signs can appear within a few hours. So can cats eat Christmas cactus flowers? They will not be poisoned, but repeated nibbling keeps upsetting the stomach. Our <a href="/tools/cat-vomiting-guide/">cat vomiting guide</a> explains which signs need a vet visit.</p>

<h3>Can eating a lot of Christmas cactus cause a blockage?</h3>
<p>Occasionally. PetMD notes that eating a larger portion raises the risk of plant material blocking the stomach or intestines. Vomiting is often an early sign, along with trouble keeping food and water down; this needs urgent veterinary care.</p>

<h3>My cat ate my Christmas cactus: what should I do?</h3>
<p>If your cat ate your Christmas cactus, stay calm: the plant is listed as non-toxic. Follow the quick steps above, offer fresh water and watch your cat. Contact a vet if vomiting continues, your cat stops eating, or you are not sure what the plant was.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/pet-poison-emergency-guide/">pet poison emergency guide</a> lists what to have ready when you call, and our checklist of <a href="/blog/signs-your-pet-is-sick/">signs your pet is sick</a> helps you judge how worried to be.</p>

<h3>What happens if a dog eats a Christmas cactus?</h3>
<p>If a dog eats Christmas cactus, the usual outcome is mild: some drooling, a single vomit or loose stools. Follow the same steps as for a cat. Call your vet if your dog ate most of the plant, keeps vomiting, or swallowed foil or fertilizer.</p>

<h2>Christmas Cactus vs Thanksgiving Cactus: Are Both Non-Toxic?</h2>
<p>Yes: all three holiday cactus types are non-toxic to cats and dogs, according to the ASPCA (Christmas cactus, with Easter cactus as an alternative name) and NC State Extension (Thanksgiving and Easter cactus). They look alike but differ in segment shape and bloom time. NC State Extension notes they are often mislabeled in stores, so check the segment edges.</p>

