<h3>Is Thanksgiving cactus toxic to cats and dogs?</h3>
<p>Thanksgiving cactus is not considered toxic to cats or dogs. The ASPCA has no separate entry for <em>Schlumbergera truncata</em>, but NC State Extension tags it non-toxic for both. Its old name, <em>Zygocactus truncatus</em>, is why it is sometimes sold as “zygocactus”.</p>

<h3>Is Easter cactus toxic to cats?</h3>
<p>Easter cactus is not considered toxic to cats or dogs. The ASPCA names “Easter cactus” as a common name in its non-toxic Christmas cactus entry, and NC State Extension tags <em>Hatiora gaertneri</em> non-toxic for cats and dogs.</p>

<h3>Are holiday cacti poisonous to cats?</h3>
<p>None of the three true holiday cacti are poisonous to cats, according to the ASPCA and NC State Extension. The bigger risk is a lookalike that only shares the word “cactus”.</p>

<h2>Lookalike Plants: When a “Cactus” Is Not Safe</h2>
<p>The pencil cactus (<em>Euphorbia tirucalli</em>), also called sticks of fire, is a spurge, not a true cactus, and the ASPCA lists it as toxic to cats, dogs and horses. Its milky sap irritates the mouth and stomach and can cause vomiting, though the ASPCA notes it is generally over-rated in toxicity.</p>
<p>Are cactus plants poisonous to cats in general? Spiny desert cacti pose a different risk: spines can lodge in a pet’s mouth or paws, so let a vet remove them. Our <a href="/tools/pet-toxic-plants-guide/">pet toxic plants guide</a> lets you check many more houseplants by name.</p>

<h2>Hidden Risks: Christmas Cactus Fertilizer, Pesticides and Pots</h2>
<p>The plant is non-toxic, but what is in the pot may not be. Pet Poison Helpline explains that fertilizers contain nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium plus trace metals such as iron, and some include herbicides, pesticides or fungicides. Small amounts usually cause stomach upset; larger amounts can cause serious poisoning.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Christmas cactus fertilizer:</strong> store it closed and out of reach.</li>
<li><strong>Bone meal or blood meal:</strong> Pet Poison Helpline notes large amounts can form a mass in the stomach, causing a blockage or pancreatitis.</li>
<li><strong>Pesticides:</strong> the ASPCA advises following label directions and keeping pets away from treated areas for as long as the label says.</li>
<li><strong>Decorations:</strong> remove foil wraps, ribbons and plant ties.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Is Christmas Cactus Poisonous to Humans, Kids or Babies?</h2>
<p>No. Tox Info Suisse, the Swiss poison information center, calls Christmas cactus a harmless plant, though contact can occasionally cause allergic skin or eye reactions. Small pieces can still be a choking hazard for babies. In the US, Poison Help is 1-800-222-1222.</p>

<h2>Is Christmas Cactus Toxic to Birds or Rabbits?</h2>
<p>Unknown: no poison-control database rates Christmas cactus for birds or rabbits, and the ASPCA covers only cats, dogs and horses. Keep it away from parrots, budgies and rabbits, and feed rabbits the hay and greens in our <a href="/blog/rabbit-care-guide-beginners/">rabbit care guide for beginners</a>.</p>

<h2>Why Is My Cat Eating My Christmas Cactus?</h2>
<p>Plant eating appears to be normal cat behavior rather than a sign of illness. A 2021 UC Davis owner survey led by Benjamin Hart found very few cats looked ill before eating plants, and younger cats ate plants most often.</p>
<p>That 2021 UC Davis owner survey (Hart et al., <em>Animals</em>) also found that 27% to 37% of cats frequently vomited after eating plants, so a cat that keeps eating Christmas cactus may be sick more often. If plant eating suddenly increases, ask your vet to check for a medical cause.</p>

<h3>How do I keep cats away from my Christmas cactus?</h3>
<p>Put the Christmas cactus somewhere your cat cannot reach, such as a hanging planter or a room that stays closed, and give your cat something better to chew.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hang it or shut it away:</strong> cats reach most shelves and windowsills.</li>
<li><strong>Offer cat grass:</strong> VCA Animal Hospitals notes many cats simply enjoy chewing grass.</li>
<li><strong>Cover the soil</strong> with large pebbles to stop digging.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/pet-proof-home-guide/">room-by-room pet-proofing guide</a> has more ideas, and if your cat targets the tree too, see <a href="/blog/how-to-cat-proof-a-christmas-tree/">how to cat-proof a Christmas tree</a>.</p>

<h2>Pet-Safe Christmas Plants vs Toxic Holiday Plants</h2>
<p>The Christmas cactus is one of the best pet-safe Christmas plants. Other non-toxic Christmas plants for cats and dogs include moth orchids and African violets. Toxic/non-toxic ratings and listed signs: ASPCA plant database. Poinsettia severity: Pet Poison Helpline. Lily kidney risk: FDA.</p>

